<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip III of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1306">1306</a>–<a href="/years/1343">1343</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Philip of Évreux</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip III (1306–1343), count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1328 jointly with his wife Joan II, daughter of Louis X of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Their accession restored a separate Navarrese monarchy after decades of union with France. Philip carried out a revision of the kingdom's laws in 1330 and died in 1343 after taking part in the Castilian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Philip III of Navarre" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acoma · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippe_III_de_Navarre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
